/*
Exercise 5 — Illustrative RLS patterns.
Adapt table/column names and validate relationships in the actual model.
Do not use this file as proof that RLS is configured.
*/

/*
Pattern A: filter a territory dimension using a security mapping table.
Assumed mapping columns:
UserAccess[UserEmail]
UserAccess[PermittedCountry]
Assumed dimension column:
DimTerritory[SalesTerritoryCountry]
*/
DimTerritory[SalesTerritoryCountry] =
    LOOKUPVALUE(
        UserAccess[PermittedCountry],
        UserAccess[UserEmail], USERPRINCIPALNAME(),
        UserAccess[PermittedCountry], DimTerritory[SalesTerritoryCountry]
    )

/*
Review notes:
- In Power BI Desktop, create a role and enter the filter expression
  in the role editor; do not paste this as a normal measure.
- Confirm the security mapping has one intended country per user, or
  choose a pattern that supports multiple permitted countries.
- Test the role with View as and then test Service access with suitable
  test accounts.
- A production design must consider relationship propagation, identity
  format, missing mappings, and least-privilege behavior.
*/
